If ${y_i (x)}$ are linearly dependent, then $W(x) = 0$ for all $x$: differentiating $sum_(i=1)^N c_i y_i = 0$ a total of $N - 1$ times gives $sum_(i=1)^N c_i bold(Y)_i (x) = bold(0)$, so the columns of the Wronskian are linearly dependent at every $x$.

It follows that if $W(x_0) != 0$ for some $x_0$, then ${y_i (x)}$ are linearly independent.

The converse fails: $W(x) = 0$ for all $x$ does not necessarily imply that ${y_i (x)}$ are linearly dependent.
